  The thing that's cheap or expensive later: if the in-app store is designed as a channel-agnostic notification record
  from the start — event happened, these are the recipients, each recipient has a per-channel delivery state — then
  adding email later is additive, one new consumer of an existing table. If in-app is instead built as "write a row,
  render a row," adding email means reworking the write path.

  My recommendation: [1min-app only now, with the record modeled channel-agnostically[0m. It gets the feature real and
  testable without a vendor, a domain reputation, or compliance work, and it doesn't paint you into a corner. Email is
  the most likely second channel and should be a deliberate second decision, not a tag-along.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which delivery channels should the first version support? → In-app inbox only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mFourth question — which changes are worth a notification.[0m

  This one is scope rather than architecture, so no long comparison. The only trap is over-generating: a notification
  for every field edit trains people to ignore the bell, and unread counts that are always non-zero are the same as no
  unread counts.

  One thing I'll assume unless you say otherwise: [1myou never get notified about your own actions.[0m Suppressing
  self-generated events at write time is standard and avoids a pointless inbox.
